ied it's hurting productivity, and you're working to cut back on social media \u2014 so I'd keep the ride audio- and reading-first rather than screen-scrolling.\n\nWant me to put together a specific daily listening/review schedule for the 40-minute ride, the way we sketched one before?", "autoeval_label": {"model": "openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": false}}
{"question_id": "caf03d32", "hypothesis": "Since you're still building your slow cooker skills (beef stew, now exploring vegan/veg options and that cashew yogurt), here are the things that make the biggest difference:\n\n**Heat & timing**\n- Low is not \"set and forget.\" Most recipes calibrated for 6\u20138 hours on low will be fine, but if you're using a newer or smaller unit, check at the 4-hour mark. Overcooked meat falls apart; undercooked tough cuts (shank, chuck) need the full time.\n- Don't lift the lid more than once. Every 10 minutes you lose roughly 30 minutes of cooking time.\n\n**Prep matters more than the cooker**\n- Sear meat (or vegetables, if you want depth) in a hot pan before adding it. That Maillard reaction is the single biggest flavor upgrade most people skip.\n- Layer with dense, slow-cooking items (root veg, grains) at the bottom and quicker-cooking things on top.\n\n**Liquid ratio**\n- Slow cookers lose very little water to evaporation. Use less liquid than a stovetop recipe \u2014 usually \u2154 to \u00be of what a pot recipe calls for. Too much liquid = stew instead of braise.\n- For thicker sauces, add a slurry (cornstarch + cold water) or a splash of soy/miso in the last 30 minutes, not at the start.\n\n**For your vegan/veg experiments**\n- Starchy veg (potatoes, carrots, squash) hold up well; delicate greens (spinach, kale, zucchini) go in during the last 30\u201345 minutes or they turn to mush.\n- Beans: use canned (rinsed) for reliability, or soak dried beans overnight and add them at the start. Unsoaked dried beans in a slow cooker can stay hard.\n- Umami is your friend: a spoon of miso, a splash of soy, nutritional yeast, or a bit of tomato paste adds the depth that meat normally provides.\n\n**Slow cooker yogurt**\n- The key is a stable, low, consistent temperature (around 42\u201345 \u00b0C / 108\u2013113 \u00b0F). Most slow cookers don't hold that precisely. If yours runs hot, the culture dies and you get a grainy mess. A yogurt maker or an Instant Pot with a yogurt function is more reliable. If you stick with the slow cooker, check the temperature with a probe and keep the lid on as much as possible.\n\n**One thing to try next time**\n- Make a simple lentil or chickpea curry: onion, garlic, ginger, cumin, a can of coconut milk, a can of tomatoes, and a can of lentils. Four hours on low. It's forgiving, tastes better the next day, and gives you a template for all the veg-based recipes you're after.\n\nWant me to look up a specific recipe or troubleshoot a particular dish you've been trying?", "autoeval_label": {"model": "openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
