{"question_id": "06f04340", "hypothesis": "Here are a few dinner ideas built around what's actually in your garden right now \u2014 cherry tomatoes, basil, mint, and peppers \u2014 and matched to what you've been cooking lately:\n\n**Top pick: Braised chicken thighs with cherry tomato and basil sauce**\nYou've been practising braising and just made a red wine braise with cremini mushrooms, so this is a natural next step. Sear chicken thighs, then braise them in a sauce of crushed cherry tomatoes, garlic, and a generous handful of torn basil. The tomatoes break down into a rich, slightly sweet sauce; the basil goes in at the end so it stays bright. Serve over the Italian herb sourdough you're developing \u2014 the rosemary and roasted garlic in it would complement the tomato-basil sauce well.\n\n**If you want something lighter: Grilled peppers and cherry tomato salad with mint-basil dressing**\nSlice the peppers (once the aphids are under control), blister or grill them, and toss with halved cherry tomatoes. Make a quick dressing with mint, basil, olive oil, lemon, and a pinch of salt. This uses all four of your homegrown ingredients and is the kind of easy, affordable plate you've said you prefer.\n\n**If you're in an Indian-cooking mood: Pepper and tomato curry with mint chutney**\nYou've been cooking Indian food at home and made a chicken tikka masala that came out well. A simple pepper-and-tomato curry (with onion, garlic, cumin, and a little cayenne) uses your peppers and cherry tomatoes as the base, and a fresh mint chutney on the side gives the mint a purpose. Serve with naan \u2014 you're already working on getting it to puff up properly, so this is a good test.\n\n**A small note on the peppers:** your pepper plants still have the aphid issue, so if you harvest from them this weekend, give the fruit a good rinse and check for any remaining insects before cooking.\n\nAll three options stay within your easy-and-affordable preference and use ingredients you already have growing. The braise is the one that most directly builds on what you've been practising in your cooking classes.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
